Hey, these are some great ideas!

> Rob Ellwood wrote in [Late-game challenges.]:

>Hallucination.  Make unicorn horns not cure hallucination.
>Move black lights to Gehennom.

Actually I like the idea of the unicorn horn curing all sorts of
illnesses; but something I think would be great to have removed is its
ability to restore lost attribute points!  The only time I ever use
potions of restore ability are 1) when I'm trying to taste-test a
potion, not knowing what it is and 2) when making holy water. 
Otherwise it's useless.  Same for the spell; when I read a book and
discover that it's RA, I just toss it into my nearby Large Box named
Trash-Compactor 3000.

>Starvation.  Gehennom is the spirit world, not the real 
>world.  No one has any reason to leave food lying around.  Also, 
>when the spirit of a creature is 'killed', there's no corpse:
>"the body of the red dragon slowly fades away".  

I don't know if I've ever gotten to Gehennom without having discovered
a ring of slow digestion.  Of course, I'd still eat something for a
possible intrinsic, but otherwise a small amount of eats in my bag
would suffice.  Regardless, though it wouldn't have much impact on my
- and I suspect others' - game, the idea of things crumbling to dust
in Gehennom appeals to me.  Just giving the place more dire effects in
general would lend itself to a more interesting aesthetic I think. 
(See below for more thoughts on crumbliness.)


>Anti-magic traps.  [snip]
>Reverse that, so that they only work on people *with* magic
>resistance.  People without magic resistance can step on them and not
>even realize there's a trap.

Ah, nice!  I like that.  Toward the end game, it seems like the only
traps I need to worry about are the fire traps, for fear of them
burning, boiling, exploding my possessions.  Land mines and pits are a
nuisance at worst.  Anything else I can imagine is resisted.  Probably
my favorite trap is the one which simultaneously blinds and spawns
monsters all around you.  I'd like to see that be more common, and
largely irresistable.  They're fun!

>Enchanting magic resistant items.  (Jakob Creutzig)  Items
>giving MR resist being enchanted 80% of the time.  

>Special case:  gray dragon scales only resist 60% of the 
>time, and you're told "the GDS glows, then resists being enchanted!"
>The message keeps new players from giving up on all dragon scales as
>worthless.

YANI.  By the time I hit Gehennom I almost always have decent weapons
above +5 and all the spiffy armor above +3.  Anything to make that a
little harder would be good.  In fact I'd like it if all items had
circumstances in which they might resist (further) enchantment. 
Uncursed enchant scrolls give an automatic +1 to an item; blessed can
give +1 to... +3 is the max, right?  What if using a blessed scroll
was a slight gamble?  Yes, it can give +3, but perhaps it could also
give nothing?  Or instead of any +, it could give an item's intrinsic
such as foo-proof, or bless it, etc.  Such a scroll should always give
something, so perhaps the pool of possible results would be foo-proof,
blessing, repairing damage, or enchantment; and if any of the first
three are already present they'd be removed from the pool?  Just
various meandering thoughts here.  But I'd love for it to be more
difficult to get really powerful items.

As for dragon armor, I know I could just play wishless, but so long as
I don't, it seems that I end up getting gray dragon scale mail very
early in the game.  The problem here is that I then have no use
whatsoever for any dragon scales I myself loot, from a dragon I myself
have killed (which is a nice feeling, to conquer some beastie and wear
its scales almost as a trophy).  Aside from resisting the temptation
of a wish for such armor, what could be done?  One idea I've had is
that perhaps the dragons should actually have a range of levels based
on their type and power of ability.

I.e., what if you couldn't even encounter gray, silver, and black
dragons until Gehennom, and their levels were increased?  What if the
yellow dragon, whose scales have no intrinsic at all, were the lowest
level of dragon and could be encountered relatively early?  The other
five dragons (red, white, orange, blue, green) could have their levels
spread throughout a range.  Perhaps the yellow dragon could appear as
soon as the last of these five dragons' babies could also appear; and
baby g/s/b dragons could appear along with the other five colors of
dragons' adults, pre-Gehennom.  Finally, in Gehennom, one can actually
see an adult g/s/b (as well as the other colors, I mean).

One other idea is that wishing for dragon scales or scale mail would
result in the spawning of a dragon wearing those scales.  You can have
'em if you can pry 'em off its dead body!

A djinni says, "All yours!"


>Blessed items.  Where blessed items now do the same thing
as unblessed items, make them just a little bit better.  Not enough
to have any significant gameplay effect, but enough to make spoiled
adventurers eagerly bless everything in sight (and possibly run low
on holy water).

I've always liked this idea. I do agree that it should only give
something a very slight improvement, or chance of special ability (TC
with a blessed teleport scroll etc.).  Maybe slight enough so that one
really has to wonder if its worth using holy water to bless it... hm,
do I have enough water?  Jeez.

Also... I haven't tried this often enough to tell: if you're praying
at your altar and wielding an unblessed weapon, there's a chance of it
becoming blessed.  Will this happen to any wielded item, regardless of
whether or not it's a weapon?  I'd like it if it were so, but I simply
don't know if this can occur or not.

Anyway: great ideas, Rob!


>Duncan Booth wrote in message [Late-game challenges.]:   

> One YANI I had a while back is sort of similar to that. Gehennom is the 
> spirit world, and we all know the saying 'you can't take it with you', so 
> when you enter or leave Gehennom, everything you are carrying (except 
> for a few things like the bell of opening) should either remain where you 
> were, or maybe crumble to dust.
> 
> Thinking about it further: when you enter or leave Gehennom everything you 
> are carrying (even in a container) gets cursed 1 (20%), 2 (75%), or 3 (5%) 
> times. If cursed when already cursed then any destroyable object crumbles 
> to dust. So you can take an artifact weapon into Gehennom with 95% safety 
> provided you bless it first, but if you want to take it back out again 
> you'll need to get holy water there. Level teleporting becomes more 
> interesting as you'll want to avoid crossing the boundary too often. You're 
> going to want an ascension kit plus a set of duplicates before the first 
> time you go down, and you'll have to decide whether to risk magicbane, 
> mjollnir or whatever. On the plus side, it becomes easier to get unholy 
> water or curse objects deliberately.

I really dig this idea of entering Gehennom being one massive curse in
itself.  Coming back out though perhaps shouldn't be?  Really, only
the descent into hell would be a curse unto itself... exiting is more
of a blessing (although shouldn't bless your items or anything).

My take on it would be to allow something to go unmolested, though:
say, not cursed (25%), cursed once (50%), cursed twice (22%), cursed
thrice (3%).  I'd want the chance of something going POOF to be not so
great; with your numbers above, an uncursed item would just poof a
great majority of the time.  Even my numbers seem a bit high to me. 
Maybe something which is blessed would be resistant to such a cursing,
leaving only uncursed and cursed items exposed.  In that case I think
something more appropriate could be: not cursed (40%), cursed once
(50%), cursed twice (10%).  Or some such.  Meaning, an already cursed
item would crumble a slight majority of the time; and an uncursed item
would crumble 10% of the time, just become cursed half the time, or
otherwise have no change.

Finding the right numbers for such an idea to be implemented would be
rather difficult, I guess.  But the idea of having to find some new
equipment in Gehennom is very appealing to me.  As it is, in Gehennom
I only check out gems, in general, just for points.  If something else
shows up as interesting using object detection and isn't far away,
I'll check it out.  But basically my only interest at that point is in
cutting my way from the staircase to staircase and continuing on down.

If, in Gehennom, I was both looking for some replacement items, and
hoping to find a (now higher level) g/s/b dragon, those maze levels
would be a bit fun to play.  As it is, though, I basically quit once I
get there.

- Phobrek, killed by a baby yellow dragon on level 6

